    "https://jobs.wisc.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-madison-wisconsin-united-states-279bfbaf-b449-48ad-a5f9-7b5a69eabe4d": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Madison, Wisconsin, United States Assistant Professor - Madison, Wisconsin, United States Skip to main content Kiosk mode Assistant Professor Requisition Number: JR10014755 Remote Type: On Site Location: Madison, Wisconsin Category: Faculty Time Type: Full time Current Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Jobs Hub to apply through the internal application process. Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Summary: The Department of Cell and Regenerative Biology in the School of Medicine and Public Health (SMPH) at the University of Wisconsin-Madison is pleased to invite applications for entry level assistant professor tenure-track faculty. We seek outstanding candidates in all areas of developmental, stem cell and regenerative biology disciplines, as the Department continues a multi-year plan for growth in this area. The successful candidate will have a primary expectation to develop and maintain an independent, externally funded research program that complements existing strengths of the department and embraces our commitment to inter-disciplinary collaboration. The successful candidate will also participate in teaching, and in professional, public and university service appropriate to the faculty rank to support the scholarly and clinical missions of UW Health and the School of Medicine and Public Health. An essential part of these duties will be working in a collegial relationship with other faculty members. We seek accomplished scientists developing independent research programs in cell biology, developmental biology, stem cell biology, and/or regenerative biology. We welcome applicants with expertise in developmental and cell biology, quantitative biology, imaging, stem cell biology, mechanisms of tissue regeneration, signaling, or mechanobiology, particularly those whose work leverages computational or AI-driven approaches. This hire will build on existing strategic strength in this area within the Department of Cell and Regenerative Biology and across the university, including at the Morgridge Institute for Research. Applications will be evaluated according to established criteria, including the quality and independence of the research program, fit with the areas above, and potential for future impact. Candidates with the most competitive applications will be invited to a videoconference interview with the search committee; those most highly rated in that stage and whose programs align well with departmental priorities, will be invited for an in-person interview. This position requires work to be completed onsite, at a designated campus work location. Key Job Responsibilities: Develop a vigorous and internationally recognized research program, 75-80% effort will be expected on research; earning and maintain extramural funding is expected. Participate in administrative and committee work to support the scholarly missions of the School of Medicine and Public Health. An essential part of these duties will be working in a collegial relationship with other faculty members. (~5% effort) Teach and mentor graduate, undergraduate and medical students within the field of Cell and Regenerative Biology (~10-15% effort) Department: School of Medicine and Public Health, Department of Cell and Regenerative Biology The fields of developmental, stem cell and regenerative biology have a longstanding tradition of excellence at the University of Wisconsin-Madison. The Department offers a stimulating and collaborative environment for research, training, and education. Junior faculty are supported by the department, school, and institution, by colleagues recognized for innovation in mentoring and mentor training and dedication to junior faculty success. UW-Madison is home to the Wisconsin Institute for Discovery and Morgridge Institute, the Wisconsin Institute for Discovery, the Center for Quantitative Cell Imaging, the Carbone Cancer Center, the Wisconsin National Primate Research Center, and the Waisman Center for Developmental Disabilities. Research is supported by modern shared facilities including the UW Optical Imaging Core, UW-Madison Flow Cytometry Laboratory, Center for High Throughput Computing, and many others. Cell and Regenerative faculty mentor graduate students in several graduate programs across campus and the department holds a new T32 for graduate training in developmental, stem cell and regenerative biology. Located on an isthmus between lakes Mendota and Monona, the city of Madison, Wisconsin is consistently ranked one of the best places to live is the US. With an extensive network of parks and bike trails, a thriving arts and cultural scene and a resilient economy, Madison is one of the fastest growing cities in the Midwest. Compensation: Negotiable, 12-month Employees in this position can expect to receive benefits such as generous vacation, holidays, and sick leave; competitive insurances and savings accounts; retirement benefits. For more information, refer to the campus benefits webpage. SMPH Faculty /Academic Staff Benefits Flyer 2026 Required Qualifications: 2-3 years of postdoctoral training in a relevant biomedical field is required by the start date of the position Record of peer-reviewed publications Education: Ph.D., MD, or MD/PhD in a relevant field is required. How to Apply: For the best experience completing your application, we recommend using Chrome or Firefox as your web browser. To apply for this position, select either “I am a current employee” or “I am not a current employee” under Apply Now. You will then be prompted to upload your application materials. Important: The application has only one attachment field. Upload the following documents in that field, either as a single combined file or as multiple files in the same upload area. Cover Letter required CV required Statement of research (not to exceed 3-pages) required Statement of teaching interests (not to exceed 1-page) required 3 letters of recommendation required Letters of recommendation are required for this application. Please instruct your references to send their letters directly by email to Jennifer Scheuer, jscheuer@wisc.edu and Carmen Schremp, ceschremp@wisc.edu by the assured consideration date of November 13, 2026. Reference letters must be received before your application can be considered complete. Your cover letter should address how your training and experience aligns with the required qualifications listed above. Application reviewers will rely on these written materials to determine which applicants move forward in the process. All applicants will be notified once the search concludes and a candidate is selected. The deadline for assuring full consideration is November 13, 2026; however, the position will remain open and applications may be considered until the position is filled. Contact Information: Dr. Ken Poss, at kposs@morgridge.org Relay Access (WTRS): 7-1-1. See RELAY_SERVICE for further information. Institutional Statement on Diversity: Diversity is a source of strength, creativity, and innovation for UW-Madison. We value the contributions of each person and respect the profound ways their identity, culture, background, experience, status, abilities, and opinion enrich the university community. We commit ourselves to the pursuit of excellence in teaching, research, outreach, and diversity as inextricably linked goals. The University of Wisconsin-Madison fulfills its public mission by creating a welcoming and inclusive community for people from every background - people who as students, faculty, and staff serve Wisconsin and the world. The University of Wisconsin-Madison is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to, including but not limited to, race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, or status as a protected veteran and other bases as defined by federal regulations and UW System policies. We promote excellence by acknowledging skills and expertise from all backgrounds and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. For more information regarding applicant and employee rights and to view federal and state required postings, visit the Human Resources Workplace Poster website . To request a disability or pregnancy-related accommodation for any step in the hiring process (e.g., application, interview, pre-employment testing, etc.), please contact the Divisional Disability Representative (DDR) in the division you are applying to. Please make your request as soon as possible to help the university respond most effectively to you. Employment may require a criminal background check. It may also require your references to answer questions regarding misconduct, including sexual violence and sexual harassment. The University of Wisconsin System will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released. See Wis. Stat. sec. 19.36(7). The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report contains current campus safety and disciplinary policies, crime statistics for the previous 3 calendar years, and on-campus student housing fire safety policies and fire statistics for the previous 3 calendar years. UW-Madison will provide a paper copy upon request; please contact the University of Wisconsin Police Department . Apply Now Are you a current employee at any University of Wisconsin campus? I am a current employee I am not a current employee Log in to Jobs Hub to apply: Please search for requisition JR10014755 in Workday Jobs Hub to get started. Log in to Workday Please enter your information below: First Name (required) f8828590 Last Name (required) 71528d68 Email address (required) 2c615ce1 Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? You will be redirected to the application to launch your career momentarily. Thank you! Questions? Frequently Asked Questions Disability Accommodations Pay Transparency Policy Statement Contact Us Refer a Friend Your First Name (required) 775d5bee Your Last Name (required) 17deca97 Your Email Address (required) 9ff952b6 Friend's Information: bcb5ce36 Send Please wait... Done Not You? You've sent this job to a friend! Website feedback, questions or accessibility issues: ohrwebmaster@ohr.wisc.edu . Learn more about accessibility at UW–Madison . © 2016–2026 Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System • Privacy Statement Before You Go.. Would You Like To Sign-up For Job Alerts? First Name (required) 699d9cee Last Name (required) 5fea3030 Email address (required) df9d3033 Job Alert Subscription f6abe34b Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Faculty Faculty Employment Type Full time Full time Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you for subscribing to UW–Madison job alerts! Close Candidate Details We use cookies on this site. By continuing to browse without changing your browser settings to block or delete cookies, you agree to the UW–Madison Privacy Notice . I do not accept I accept Please choose the employment type(s)"
