    "https://jobs.wisc.edu/jobs/assistant-associate-or-full-professor-of-law-state-and-local-democracy-madison-wisconsin-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant, Associate or full Professor of Law (State and Local Democracy)",
      "description": "Assistant, Associate or full Professor of Law (State and Local Democracy) - Madison, Wisconsin, United States Assistant, Associate or full Professor of Law (State and Local Democracy) - Madison, Wisconsin, United States Skip to main content Kiosk mode Assistant, Associate or full Professor of Law (State and Local Democracy) Requisition Number: JR10013295 Remote Type: On Site Location: Madison, Wisconsin Category: Faculty Current Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Jobs Hub to apply through the internal application process. Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Summary: This position will serve as a tenure-track or tenured faculty member at the University of Wisconsin Law School. We are recruiting a faculty member to conduct research and teach classes in one or more of the following areas: constitutional law, state constitutional law, local government law, property and zoning law, election law, voting rights, and legislation and regulation. As a faculty member, the person holding this position will also engage in service to the Law School as well as to the University and the profession. Key Job Responsibilities: Conduct research and teach classes in one or more of the following areas: constitutional law, state constitutional law, local government law, property or zoning law, election law, voting rights, and legislation and regulation. Engage in service of the type expected of faculty members. Department: University of Wisconsin Law School Compensation: Minimum 9-month salary of $150,000, but starting title and salary will be commensurate with successful candidate's previous experience, skills and qualifications. Previous teaching experience required to be hired at the associate or full professor levels. Required Qualifications: Qualified applicants will have a record of outstanding scholarly achievement, or the potential for such achievement, in the study of governance, constitutional law, and democratic institutions at the state and local levels. The person holding this position will be encouraged to collaborate with dynamic campus centers including the State Democracy Research Initiative. Subject areas of focus may include state constitutional law; local government and governance; property and zoning law; election law; voting rights; and legislation and regulation. The Law School seeks to recruit outstanding faculty who demonstrate excellence or strong potential in research and teaching, and who are likely to enrich our community. Candidates may do so by engaging with the Wisconsin Idea, building on existing strengths, introducing new areas of substantive or methodological focus, and/or advancing our commitment to pluralism and healthy civil discourse. Additional Information: The University of Wisconsin Law School, located in Madison, WI, is an integral part of the University of Wisconsin-Madison campus, one of the top research universities in the United States. It offers both JD and advanced law degrees, and enrolls students from around the United States and around the world. Madison is a city of lakes, frequently listed among the top ten places to live in the United States. Further information about the law school is available at www.law.wisc.edu . Education: J.D. or equivalent degree required. How to Apply: Applicants must apply through the Jobs at UW website ( www.jobs.wisc.edu ). To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by October 1, 2026 . Applications will continue to be accepted until the position is filled. Please submit a cover letter (optional) and curriculum vitae (CV) as a single combined document. Contact Information: For any questions, please email the Faculty Committee at fac@law.wisc.edu Institutional Statement on Diversity: Diversity is a source of strength, creativity, and innovation for UW-Madison. We value the contributions of each person and respect the profound ways their identity, culture, background, experience, status, abilities, and opinion enrich the university community. We commit ourselves to the pursuit of excellence in teaching, research, outreach, and diversity as inextricably linked goals. The University of Wisconsin-Madison fulfills its public mission by creating a welcoming and inclusive community for people from every background - people who as students, faculty, and staff serve Wisconsin and the world. The University of Wisconsin-Madison is an Equal Opportunity Employer. Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to, including but not limited to, race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, or status as a protected veteran and other bases as defined by federal regulations and UW System policies. We promote excellence by acknowledging skills and expertise from all backgrounds and encourage all qualified individuals to apply. For more information regarding applicant and employee rights and to view federal and state required postings, visit the Human Resources Workplace Poster website . To request a disability or pregnancy-related accommodation for any step in the hiring process (e.g., application, interview, pre-employment testing, etc.), please contact the Divisional Disability Representative (DDR) in the division you are applying to. Please make your request as soon as possible to help the university respond most effectively to you. Employment may require a criminal background check. It may also require your references to answer questions regarding misconduct, including sexual violence and sexual harassment. The University of Wisconsin System will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except that the identity of the successful candidate will be released. See Wis. Stat. sec. 19.36(7). The Annual Security and Fire Safety Report contains current campus safety and disciplinary policies, crime statistics for the previous 3 calendar years, and on-campus student housing fire safety policies and fire statistics for the previous 3 calendar years. UW-Madison will provide a paper copy upon request; please contact the University of Wisconsin Police Department . Apply Now Are you a current employee at any University of Wisconsin campus? I am a current employee I am not a current employee Log in to Jobs Hub to apply: Please search for requisition JR10013295 in Workday Jobs Hub to get started. Log in to Workday Please enter your information below: First Name (required) 3dd3bb85 Last Name (required) 85633432 Email address (required) 3fe48a4a Apply Now Please wait... Done Not You? You will be redirected to the application to launch your career momentarily. Thank you! Questions? Frequently Asked Questions Disability Accommodations Pay Transparency Policy Statement Contact Us Refer a Friend Your First Name (required) 537c1978 Your Last Name (required) f7b33a6b Your Email Address (required) ebff4fee Friend's Information: 9676e47f Send Please wait... Done Not You? You've sent this job to a friend! Website feedback, questions or accessibility issues: ohrwebmaster@ohr.wisc.edu . Learn more about accessibility at UW–Madison . © 2016–2026 Board of Regents of the University of Wisconsin System • Privacy Statement Before You Go.. Would You Like To Sign-up For Job Alerts? First Name (required) 2a662575 Last Name (required) 34bf0e83 Email address (required) e08182b8 Job Alert Subscription 425e2212 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories Faculty Faculty Employment Type Subscribe Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you for subscribing to UW–Madison job alerts! Close Candidate Details We use cookies on this site. By continuing to browse without changing your browser settings to block or delete cookies, you agree to the UW–Madison Privacy Notice . I do not accept I accept Please choose the employment type(s)"
